<header class="masthead">
  <p class="series-label"><span>Chokepoint Geopolitics Research · Paper II</span> <span style="opacity:0.6;font-size:0.55rem;letter-spacing:0.1em;"> | Paper I: <em>The Tungsten Clock</em></span></p>
  <h1>The Architecture of <em>Denial</em></h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Iran's Coupled Maritime Doctrine at Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb as a Form of Governance</p>
  <div class="brass-rule"></div>
  <p class="byline"><strong>Beniam Awash, PhD</strong><br>Working Paper · April 2026 · Updated 9 July 2026</p>
</header>

<div style="background:#1A1208;border-top:1px solid #3A2A10;border-bottom:1px solid #3A2A10;padding:0.7rem 2rem;text-align:center;">
  <p style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.62rem;color:#8A7A60;letter-spacing:0.06em;line-height:1.7;margin:0;">
    <strong style="color:#B5832A;">Working Paper</strong> — This paper is circulated for discussion and comment. It represents the author's analysis as of 9 July 2026 based on open-source reporting, primary documents, and independent research. It has not undergone formal peer review. Claims are sourced and tiered according to the evidence taxonomy below. The paper will be updated as events develop. Comments and corrections are welcome. <strong style="color:#8A7A60;">Views expressed are the author's own and do not represent those of any institution.</strong>
  </p>
</div>

<div class="exec-summary">
  <div class="exec-inner">
    <span class="exec-label">Executive Summary</span>
    <p>Iran has constructed a coupled maritime denial architecture at the Strait of Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb that functions as governance rather than warfare. The Strait is not being blockaded — it is being administered. That is the central analytical frame here, built on a documented operational record and interpreted, not merely described, below. The toll is not a ransom — it is a tariff. The IRGC's selective passage regime, codified by Iran's Majlis committee in March 2026 and institutionalized through the Persian Gulf Strait Authority (PGSA) in May 2026, suggests the emergence of chokepoint sovereignty: a form of territorial authority that existing international law, UNCLOS doctrine, and freedom-of-navigation frameworks appear poorly designed to address.</p>
    <p>The paper develops the framing of <em>tollbooth as governance architecture</em> and situates it within Farrell and Newman's weaponized interdependence framework — arguing that Iran has inverted the asymmetry, converting a Western coercive instrument into a platform for durable structural leverage. The IRGC's May 4 release of a new "control map" — expanding the administered zone from approximately 32–48 km to 322–483 km in effective width and placing over 22,000 sq km under PGSA jurisdiction, including areas within Oman's and the UAE's territorial waters — converts the tollbooth from an operational claim into a cartographic one. The paper closes with analysis of the April 8 ceasefire and June 18 Islamabad MOU as recognition rather than resolution, and an assessment of what would actually have to change for the architecture to fail.</p>
    <ul class="key-claims">
      <li>Iran's Hormuz toll regime is a governance architecture, not a military instrument. It requires negotiation with the governing authority, not its defeat.</li>
      <li>Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb interact under specific routing conditions: when the Cape of Good Hope detour is available but costly, disruption at one chokepoint forecloses the cost-mitigation option the other would otherwise provide. Iran and its Houthi partners hold coupled, though organizationally distinct, leverage over both.</li>
      <li>The Persian Gulf Strait Authority (PGSA), founded May 5, 2026, processed 300+ transit requests within weeks of launch, established a public-facing X account, and was formally sanctioned by OFAC — none of which is consistent with a temporary wartime measure.</li>
      <li>On May 4, the IRGC published a "control map" expanding the administered area of the Strait ten-fold — placing 22,000+ sq km, including portions of Oman's and the UAE's territorial waters, under PGSA jurisdiction. The UAE called it "fragments of dreams." The map was not retracted.</li>
      <li>The April 8 ceasefire did not dismantle the toll regime. The June 18 Islamabad MOU — a non-binding statement of mutual intent, not a legal instrument — operationalizes interaction with the PGSA rather than dissolving it. Speaker Ghalibaf was categorical: "The Strait of Hormuz will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran, in accordance with international law."</li>
      <li>On June 19, the PGSA circulated a mandatory insurance requirement for all transiting vessels — free during the 60-day MOU window, with fees explicitly reserved for day 61. After approximately August 17, 2026, PGSA insurance fees would require transacting with an OFAC-designated entity.</li>
      <li>Yuan- and crypto-denominated toll payments functionally convert each Hormuz transit into a marginal de-dollarization event, regardless of the vessel's flag.</li>
      <li>The architecture's durability is testable, not assumed. None of the three conditions that would dislodge it — naval interdiction of cleared vessels, insurer refusal, or defection by major non-adversary users — has occurred in the system's first ninety days.</li>
      <li>On June 29, Foreign Minister Araghchi asserted that Iran alone bears responsibility for reopening the Strait within 30 days and warned against any "separate arrangements" — a sole-authority claim stronger than the toll regime alone implied. The same week, 68 of 88 members of Iran's Assembly of Experts publicly pressured the MOU negotiators to treat Hormuz management as a non-negotiable "religious obligation," confirming that the architecture now carries embedded domestic political investment independent of the external bargain with Washington.</li>
      <li>On June 29, Iran and Oman convened the first formal meeting of a joint Strait of Hormuz governance committee in Muscat, explicitly invoked under Article 5 of the Islamabad MOU — converting the informal bilateral arrangement traced from Section I onward into a standing institutional body.</li>
      <li>In a July statement to the IMO, Oman rejected compulsory Hormuz transit fees as incompatible with international law — a direct legal contestation of the toll regime's core logic from the very state whose bilateral cooperation had been treated above as evidence of the claimed zone's operative status. The joint committee has not been dissolved, but "conformity without contestation" no longer describes Oman's position.</li>
      <li>On July 8, the Islamabad MOU collapsed after the United States deliberately escorted Saudi, Qatari, and UAE-linked tankers through the southern Omani corridor without PGSA clearance — a strategy designed to contest Iranian jurisdictional control of the Strait while nominally remaining within the MOU framework. Iran attacked those tankers, and Trump declared the MOU "over." Former French Ambassador Araud confirmed publicly that "at the first reading by any diplomat, the MoU was obviously giving Iran a free hand in the strait of Hormuz." Despite the MOU's collapse, the PGSA remains operational and the U.S. has not reinstated the naval blockade of Iranian ports. The governance architecture continues to function without the agreement that legitimized it — the architecture of denial has now survived not just a ceasefire but its collapse.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="evidence-key">
  <span class="evidence-key-label">Evidence Taxonomy</span>
  <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span><span class="tier-def">Primary source, official statement, or multiple independent outlets</span>
  <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span><span class="tier-def">Single credible source, not yet corroborated</span>
  <span class="tier tier-e">Estimated</span><span class="tier-def">Derived from verified data via established methodology</span>
  <span class="tier tier-i">Analyst Inference</span><span class="tier-def">Author's interpretive judgment, clearly flagged</span>
</div>

<div style="background:var(--sand);border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1rem 2rem;">
  <div style="max-width:740px;margin:0 auto;">
    <p style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.62rem;letter-spacing:0.04em;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.8;margin:0;">
      <strong style="color:var(--ink);">Methodology &amp; Evidence Standards —</strong>
      This paper draws on four source categories, each tagged inline using the evidence taxonomy above.
      <strong>Confirmed</strong> claims rest on primary documents, official statements, or corroboration by two or more independent credible outlets (Lloyd's List Intelligence, U.S. Treasury/OFAC press releases, Majlis legislative records, ICJ case text).
      <strong>Reported</strong> claims derive from single credible sources not yet independently corroborated.
      <strong>Estimated</strong> claims use documented data and established methodology.
      <strong>Analyst Inference</strong> marks the author's interpretive judgments, clearly distinguished from empirical claims throughout.
      One source (diplomatic contact, March 2026) is anonymized at the contact's request. Readers are encouraged to follow footnote links directly to primary sources where available.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
