<!-- SECTION V: TOLLBOOTH AS GOVERNANCE -->
<div class="section" id="s5">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">V</span><h2>The Governance Model: <em>Tollbooth as Architecture</em></h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: A blockade is an act of war. A toll road is an administrative instrument. Iran has performed the latter — and international institutions have no doctrine for it.</div>
  <p>The concept I want to introduce here — and which I argue is the paper's primary analytical contribution — is <em>tollbooth as governance architecture</em>.</p>
  <p>A blockade is an act of war. It is temporary, directed at a specific adversary, and ends when the adversary capitulates or the blocking power withdraws. International law has developed doctrines for governing blockades — from the 1856 Declaration of Paris through the San Remo Manual. The institutions that respond to blockades — the UNSC, naval task forces, diplomatic pressure campaigns — are calibrated for this model.</p>
  <p>A toll road is none of these things. It is permanent rather than temporary. It is non-discriminatory in form — any vessel may transit, provided the toll is paid — even when discriminatory in practice, because the toll schedule is structured to impose prohibitive costs on adversary-flagged or adversary-destined shipping. It generates revenue rather than merely imposes cost. It requires not the defeat of the governing authority but negotiation with it. And crucially: international legal frameworks have no adequate doctrine for a toll road imposed at a globally critical maritime chokepoint by a state claiming jurisdictional authority.</p>
  <p>Elinor Ostrom's work on the governance of commons is normally invoked to explain how communities manage shared resources collectively, without either privatizing them or relying on top-down state coercion.<sup id="ref11a"><a href="#fn11a">[11a]</a></sup> The <em>Ostrom inversion</em> is the correct analytical frame for what Iran has done at Hormuz. It runs that logic in reverse: instead of averting enclosure through collective management, Iran has enclosed a global commons outright, converting an international strait into a controlled-access regime. It has done so, notably, without the formal apparatus of privatization that enclosure normally requires.<sup id="ref11b"><a href="#fn11b">[11b]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span> The commons is now a toll road. The question is no longer how to defend freedom of navigation. It is whether to buy a pass.</p>
  <table class="tier-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Nation Category</th><th>Transit Status</th><th>Payment Mechanism</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Iran/Oman bilateral protocol signatories</td><td>Free passage</td><td>None required</td><td><span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>"Friendly" states (China, India, Russia, Pakistan)</td><td>Tolled passage; bilateral carve-outs emerging</td><td>~$2M/VLCC; yuan, Bitcoin, USDT via PGSA</td><td><span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Non-aligned developing states</td><td>Conditional tolled passage</td><td>Reduced rate; updated list</td><td><span class="tier tier-e">Estimated</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Active sanctions states (US, UK, EU, Japan, Korea)</td><td>Denied — no fee, no corridor, no passage</td><td>N/A</td><td><span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Israel-linked vessels</td><td>Denied at any price</td><td>N/A</td><td><span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Oil tanker fees are documented at approximately $0.50–$1.00 per barrel of crude cargo — meaning a fully loaded VLCC carrying two million barrels pays approximately $2 million per transit. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Container ship fees are negotiated individually.<sup id="ref12"><a href="#fn12">[12]</a></sup> The list of "good" and "bad" actors, in the PGSA's own operational practice, is continuously updated. This is not the language of warfare. It is the language of regulation. The UNSC has procedures for condemning acts of war. It has no procedure for auditing a toll schedule.</p>
  <p>What converts capability into governance is the presence of <em>rules applied to non-adversaries</em>: a published-in-practice tier structure, a documented application and clearance process, a payment infrastructure built for repeat transactions rather than one-off extraction, and continuity of the system across diplomatic crises rather than a single confrontation. The PGSA's processing of several hundred non-adversary transit applications is the empirical fact that does the work here: adversary vessels excluded categorically, non-adversary vessels processed administratively, the distinction sustained over months rather than days.</p>
  <p>Three claims are embedded in this argument and carry different evidentiary weight. The routing of vessels through the IRGC-controlled Larak Island corridor is <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> by Lloyd's List Intelligence and multiple corroborating maritime sources. The payment of fees for passage — at least two vessels documented by Lloyd's List, with yuan settlement — is <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span> by a single credible specialist outlet and not yet independently corroborated in full detail. The characterization of this operational pattern as <em>governance</em> rather than episodic coercion is <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span> — an interpretive claim built on the documented record, not equivalent to it.</p>
  <p>The strongest objection to this argument is not that the facts are wrong but that the framing is too generous: that what is being described is extortion conducted with administrative manners, and that duress does not become legitimate merely because it is organized. The U.S. Treasury's own characterization of the PGSA — an IRGC revenue mechanism extorting commercial shipping — is precisely this objection, made by the actor with the strongest interest in rejecting the governance framing.<sup id="ref12a"><a href="#fn12a">[12a]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> That objection is taken seriously here, and its premise is not disputed. The toll regime may well be extortion under international law. None of that is in question.</p>
  <p>What is in question is a separate matter: whether a system can simultaneously be illegitimate under international law and function, as a matter of observable practice, as the operative governance arrangement for a critical chokepoint. These are not mutually exclusive. Apartheid-era South Africa's control of Walvis Bay — condemned by UN Security Council Resolution 432 (1978) as an attempted annexation inconsistent with Namibian territorial integrity, yet exercised as functional sovereignty over the port until 1994 — is the clearest precedent for the distinction between legal status and operational governance.<sup id="ref11c"><a href="#fn11c">[11c]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Legal illegitimacy describes the status of a claim; operational governance describes what happens to ships. The policy-relevant question addressed here is the second one.</p>
</div>
<!-- SECTION VI: LEGAL ARCHITECTURE -->
<div class="section" id="s6">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">VI</span><h2>The Legal Architecture: UNCLOS and Its Discontents</h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: Iran's framing exploits the gap between innocent passage, which permits coastal-state regulation, and transit passage, which does not — a gap that the Corfu Channel precedent, decided on prohibition rather than conditional passage, does not close. Both Iran and the US are UNCLOS non-parties, leaving no compulsory forum to resolve it.</div>
  <p>The legal basis for Iran's position is contested but not frivolous. In short: transit passage forbids tolls; innocent passage permits a coastal state to regulate. Iran's legal strategy is an attempt to have Hormuz treated as the second when international consensus has long treated it as the first.</p>
  <div class="analytic-box" data-label="Reading Guide — Three Distinct Claims">
    <h3>What Iran Claims, What the Law Says, What Follows Analytically</h3>
    <p>Three different kinds of statement are in play here, and conflating them is the most common error in commentary on the Hormuz toll regime. They are kept separate below. <strong>What Iran claims:</strong> that the Strait falls under a regulatory regime closer to innocent passage than transit passage, that fees are compensation for "specialized services" rather than charges on passage itself, and that the Iran-Oman bilateral protocol supplies an independent jurisdictional basis.<sup id="ref13"><a href="#fn13">[13]</a></sup> These are assertions by an interested party, reported as such — not treated here as established law. <strong>What the law says:</strong> UNCLOS Article 38 codifies transit passage as the applicable doctrine for straits used for international navigation, and the Corfu Channel precedent supports a right of passage that the coastal state may not prohibit. But Corfu Channel addressed prohibition, not conditional fee-for-passage, leaving the specific legal question Iran's framing raises unresolved by existing doctrine. No tribunal with compulsory jurisdiction over either party has ruled on it. <strong>What follows analytically:</strong> regardless of how the legal question is eventually resolved — if it ever is — the PGSA's operational record (documented application processing, tiered non-adversary treatment, sustained operation across diplomatic phases) constitutes a separate, descriptive claim about <em>de facto</em> governance that does not depend on the legal claim being correct. A regime can be unlawful and still be the operative arrangement vessels, insurers, and states must respond to. The normative position here is limited to that descriptive claim: not that Iran's legal theory is correct, only that its operational practice has produced effects requiring analysis on its own terms.</p>
  </div>
  <p>UNCLOS distinguishes between two regimes. <em>Innocent passage</em>, the older customary doctrine, permits a coastal state meaningful regulatory latitude — it may require notification, restrict the passage of warships, and suspend passage temporarily for security reasons, provided the suspension is non-discriminatory. <em>Transit passage</em>, codified in UNCLOS Article 38 for straits used for international navigation, is a stronger right: continuous and expeditious passage that the coastal state may not hamper, suspend, or burden with charges for the mere fact of passing through.<sup id="ref13a"><a href="#fn13a">[13a]</a></sup> Iran's position — articulated through Araghchi's framing and the Majlis legislation — functions as a claim to regulate under something closer to the innocent-passage standard, recharacterized as "specialized services" subject to fees.</p>
  <p>The foundational precedent here is the Corfu Channel case (ICJ, 1949). The ICJ held that states have a right, in time of peace, to send vessels through straits connecting two parts of the high seas without prior authorization from the coastal state, and that absent a specific international convention to the contrary, a coastal state may not <em>prohibit</em> such passage.<sup id="ref13b"><a href="#fn13b">[13b]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> But Corfu Channel addressed prohibition of passage — not a regime that permits passage conditional on payment. Iran's framing is constructed precisely to occupy that gap: it does not prohibit transit, and Tehran's position is that it charges not for passage as such, but for ancillary services — escort, routing information, "geopolitical vetting" — that the conflict itself has made necessary. Whether that framing survives scrutiny is exactly the question Corfu Channel does not answer.</p>
  <p>A further complication: both principal antagonists are non-parties to UNCLOS. Iran has not ratified the Convention, and neither has the United States — though Washington has consistently maintained that the transit passage regime has crystallized into binding customary international law applicable even to non-parties.<sup id="ref13c"><a href="#fn13c">[13c]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span> The result is two states each invoking the same instrument as authoritative, in mutually contradictory directions, while neither is bound by its text. There is no court of compulsory jurisdiction either party has consented to use, and the customary-law arguments on each side are addressed to an audience of third states and insurers rather than to a tribunal that will rule.</p>
  <p>Iran's Majlis lawmaker Mohammadreza Rezaei Kouchi stated the logic plainly: "We provide its security, and it is natural that ships and oil tankers should pay such fees." <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The bill before parliament explicitly bars all US-linked and Israeli-linked vessels from any transit option. No fee, no corridor, no passage.<sup id="ref14"><a href="#fn14">[14]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> As of April 3, 2026, no international tribunal had accepted the principle that a coastal state may charge for transit through a natural international strait on the basis of services rendered — services the transiting vessel did not request and does not need absent the threat created by the coastal state itself.<sup id="ref15"><a href="#fn15">[15]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p>Professor James Kraska of the Naval War College has written that the right of transit passage is a right against the coastal state. Its enforcement, however, depends ultimately on the willingness of major naval powers to defend it militarily.<sup id="ref16"><a href="#fn16">[16]</a></sup> Professor Jason Chuah of City University London has analyzed the insurance law dimensions of the same problem. Lloyd's List Intelligence data confirms vessels are paying tolls to transit; whether those payments constitute extortion or a legitimate tariff is a legal question that cannot be resolved without a primary jurisdiction willing to adjudicate it.<sup id="ref17"><a href="#fn17">[17]</a></sup> That jurisdiction has not yet materialized.</p>
  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 8 June 2026';">
    <p>On 8 June 2026, the European Union imposed sanctions on the IRGC Navy's Hormozgan Provincial Command and two individuals — Mohammad Akbarzadeh (Deputy Commander for Political Affairs, IRGC Navy) and Hamid Hosseini — marking the first time the EU has used new powers specifically to sanction Iran for restricting freedom of maritime navigation. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p>This is analytically significant for three reasons. First, it adds a third major sanctioning body to the architecture — alongside U.S. Treasury/OFAC (May 27) — confirming a converging multilateral legal response. Second, the EU's stated legal basis ("threatening freedom of maritime traffic") implicitly accepts the transit-passage framework as the applicable standard, directly contesting Iran's reclassification attempt. Third, the EU sanctioned the provincial IRGC command rather than the PGSA itself, which may reflect uncertainty about the PGSA's precise organizational status. Notably, Akbarzadeh — the individual sanctioned by the EU — was also the IRGC official who publicly announced the geographic expansion of the PGSA control map on May 4 (see Section VII below). The EU's designation of him by name confirms the centrality of the cartographic claim to the enforcement response.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION VII: THE REMAPPED STRAIT -->
<div class="section" id="s5b">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">VII</span><h2>The Remapped Strait: <em>Cartographic Sovereignty</em></h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: On May 4, 2026, the IRGC released a "control map" expanding the administered area of the Strait ten-fold — from approximately 32–48 km to 322–483 km in effective width. The map alone is a unilateral assertion; this section identifies the specific additional facts that would, and currently partly do, convert it from assertion into an institutionally operative claim.</div>
  <p>A toll road requires a gate. On May 4, 2026, Iran published one — in cartographic form.</p>
  <p>Mohammad Akbarzadeh, Deputy Political Director of the IRGC Navy, announced to Iran's state media: "In the past, the Strait of Hormuz was defined as a limited area around islands such as Hormuz and Hengam, but today this view has changed."<sup id="refM1"><a href="#fnM1">[M1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The new IRGC map placed the "Strait of Hormuz area controlled and managed by Iran's armed forces" between two lines: east, from Kuh-e Mubarak on Iran's coast to south of Fujairah in the UAE; west, from the tip of Qeshm Island to Umm Al Quwain in the UAE. The resulting zone covers more than 22,000 square kilometres — extending into the territorial waters of both Oman and the UAE, states not party to the bilateral Iran-Oman arrangement and not consulted on the expansion.</p>
  <div class="geo-stats">
    <div class="geo-stat">
      <span class="geo-stat-val">10<em>×</em></span>
      <span class="geo-stat-label">Effective expansion<br>of administered width<br>32–48 km → 322–483 km</span>
      <span class="geo-stat-tier">▪ CONFIRMED · IRGC/Fars, May 4 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="geo-stat">
      <span class="geo-stat-val">22<em>K+</em></span>
      <span class="geo-stat-label">Square kilometres<br>under PGSA jurisdiction<br>per IRGC control map</span>
      <span class="geo-stat-tier">▪ CONFIRMED · IRGC control map</span>
    </div>
    <div class="geo-stat">
      <span class="geo-stat-val">2</span>
      <span class="geo-stat-label">Sovereign states<br>whose territorial waters<br>the map overlaps (UAE, Oman)</span>
      <span class="geo-stat-tier">▪ CONFIRMED · UNCLOS coordinates</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- MAP FIGURE: PLACEHOLDER FOR IRAN CONTROL MAP IMAGE -->
  <figure class="map-figure" id="irgc-control-map">
    <div class="map-figure-label">FIGURE 3 · IRGC STRAIT OF HORMUZ CONTROL MAP · Released May 4, 2026</div>
    <img src="hormuz-irgc-control-map.jpg" alt="IRGC control map showing expanded Strait of Hormuz jurisdiction, released May 4, 2026">
    <figcaption>
      <strong>Source: IRGC Navy / Fars News Agency, May 4, 2026.</strong> The map expands the IRGC's claimed administrative area ten-fold relative to the pre-conflict Strait definition. The UAE described Iran's claimed lines as "nothing but fragments of dreams" — a sovereign-level rejection of the map's legal basis that, as of this writing, has not changed vessel routing or transit processing within the claimed zone. The map has not been retracted and continues to function as the geographic basis the PGSA's transit-authorization regime operates on in practice, distinct from the unresolved question of its legal validity. The 80 mines estimated in the central passage route further enforce the re-channeling of all transit into the two alternate corridors — the northern (Iranian, through Larak Island) and southern (Omani) routes — both of which fall within the PGSA-claimed zone.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="pull-quote">
    <p>The Strait of Hormuz will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran, in accordance with international law.</p>
    <cite>— Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf, Iranian Parliament Speaker and chief nuclear negotiator, returning from Buergenstock talks, June 2026<sup id="refM4"><a href="#fnM4">[M4]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></cite>
  </div>

  <p>Speaker Ghalibaf's statement — made in a video posted to his Telegram account after the first round of U.S.-Iran talks — is the most categorical public articulation Iran has offered of the architecture's intended permanence. Whether that intention has yet become institutional fact is precisely what the rest of this section examines, not something the statement itself settles. What the statement does establish is narrower and more useful: Iran's own negotiators are not treating the pre-war status quo — in which no state claimed administrative jurisdiction over transit and no authority processed applications for passage — as an available negotiated outcome. The negotiations underway concern the terms of whatever arrangement succeeds it, not a return to the prior baseline.</p>

  <p>The case for taking that ambition seriously rather than dismissing it as posturing — and for treating the underlying claim as a meaningful institutional fact rather than mere assertion — is built across the rest of this section.</p>

  <div class="analytic-box" data-label="Analytical Assessment — From Assertion to Institutional Fact">
    <h3>What the Map Does That the Toll Didn't — And What It Doesn't Do By Itself</h3>
    <p>A map, by itself, is cheap. Iran's publication of the control map on May 4 is, on its own, no more institutionally binding than any unilateral cartographic claim a state has ever issued — and several such claims (most maritime boundary disputes, for instance) persist for decades without producing operative jurisdiction. The map does not become "cartographic sovereignty" merely by existing or by going unretracted. The bridge from map to institutional fact rests on three additional, separately verifiable conditions — not on the map alone.</p>
    <p><strong>Condition one: third-party behavioral conformity.</strong> Vessels, insurers, and at least one other littoral state must act as though the claimed zone is operative, independent of whether they accept its legal basis. On the operational side, this is <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span>: the PGSA's transit-application process — documented operationally by the independent ABC News test described in Section XII below — routes non-adversary vessels through the claimed zone via the Larak Island corridor, and the June 29 Iran-Oman joint committee (Section X) addresses "management" of the strait under Article 5 of the Islamabad MOU. But this condition should not be read as settled: Oman's July statement to the IMO explicitly rejects compulsory transit fees as incompatible with international law (Section X, Update · 9 July), which is a direct contestation of the fee logic even as the joint committee itself continues to operate. Behavioral conformity at the level of institutional participation and legal acceptance of the fee regime are not the same thing, and the evidence now points in different directions on each.</p>
    <p><strong>Condition two: absence of sustained contestation that changes the operative facts.</strong> The UAE's "fragments of dreams" statement is real and is the strongest available counter-evidence to the cartographic-sovereignty claim — it is direct, public, sovereign-level rejection of the map's legal basis. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> What it has not done, as of this writing, is change vessel routing, insurance terms, or transit processing in the claimed zone. The distinction is narrow and worth stating precisely. Iran has not won the legal argument over the map, and the UAE's rejection keeps that argument open. But the map's claimed boundaries continue to function as the de facto routing geography regardless of who wins it. That is a claim about operational practice, not about legal title, and the two should not be conflated.</p>
    <p><strong>Condition three: integration with an existing payment and authorization infrastructure.</strong> The map is not a freestanding claim; it defines the geographic scope of the PGSA's application-and-clearance system documented in Sections V and XII. A map without an authorization bureaucracy behind it would be a much weaker claim, more easily dismissed as posturing. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>What would falsify the cartographic-sovereignty reading:</strong> if non-adversary vessels and their insurers began routinely transiting the claimed zone without PGSA clearance, without consequence, and without compensating war-risk pricing — i.e., if condition one above stopped holding — the map would revert to being a rhetorical claim rather than an operative one. That has not happened in the period covered here, but it is the specific, observable test, and it has not been run to exhaustion either.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Intertanko marine director Phil Belcher's description of current shipping operations captures condition one in practice: "The main route… through the middle of the Strait of Hormuz, that's closed, that's dangerous… This is like a highway where the road in the middle is closed, and you are using the hard shoulder. We need to get the highway open so we can get the volume of traffic through safely. One of the big issues we've got at the moment is the navigational risk, the risk of running aground on the rocks."<sup id="refM5"><a href="#fnM5">[M5]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The "hard shoulder" runs through the zone the PGSA map has claimed, and using it functions as engagement with PGSA-administered space whether or not the vessel has filed a formal application. This is best read as coercive practice that has, so far, gone unrefused by the vessels subject to it — not as evidence that the underlying jurisdictional question has been settled in Iran's favor. The 80 mines estimated in the central route, also reported by Belcher and confirmed by Lloyd's List, reinforce the same point from the physical side: they do not distinguish between adversary and non-adversary vessels, and they function as an infrastructure of compulsion that routes all transit through the lanes the IRGC controls, regardless of flag or fee status. The physical architecture and the administrative architecture are not parallel systems. They are the same system.</p>
</div>
<!-- SECTION VIII: DE-DOLLARIZATION -->
<div class="section" id="s7">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">VIII</span><h2>The Financial Architecture: De-Dollarization as a Feature, Not a Bug</h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: Yuan- and crypto-denominated toll payments functionally convert each Hormuz transit into a marginal de-dollarization event. The June 19 PGSA mandatory insurance requirement is the next iteration of the same logic.</div>
  <p>The toll regime's payment infrastructure is not incidental to its strategic logic. It is the strategy. By denominating transit tolls in yuan, Bitcoin, and USDT, Iran has built a payment mechanism that functions, transaction by transaction, as a non-dollar settlement channel at global scale. Each vessel that pays a Hormuz toll completes a transaction in a currency other than the dollar — individually marginal, but repeated across hundreds of transits and embedded in the infrastructure that the petrodollar architecture has anchored U.S. financial power upon since the 1970s Kissinger-Saudi arrangement.<sup id="ref18"><a href="#fn18">[18]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  <p>As TRM Labs documented in April 2026, the IRGC had already routed approximately $1 billion through offshore stablecoin infrastructure before the war — exploiting low transaction costs, high throughput, deep broker liquidity, and widespread regional adoption, all outside the U.S. correspondent banking system.<sup id="ref19a"><a href="#fn19">[19]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> That the Hormuz toll system deployed the same underlying architecture — transforming a pre-existing sanctions-evasion rail into a real-time revenue collection mechanism for the Iranian state — is an inference connecting the TRM Labs finding to the toll regime, not a claim TRM Labs makes directly.<sup id="ref19"><a href="#fn19">[19]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  <p>The downstream effect on Asian sanctioning-state calculations is the architecture's most elegant feature. Japan, South Korea, and EU member states currently applying Iran sanctions face a choice: maintain the sanctions and pay prohibitive freight costs, or relax the sanctions and gain access to the tolled-but-navigable corridor. The architecture has converted the sanctions coalition from a collective action success into a collective action problem. The April 3, 2026 transit by France's CMA CGM Kribi — timed to coincide with Paris's Security Council veto of the Chapter VII resolution — demonstrated precisely how Iran's Hormuz sorting mechanism is designed to peel Western nations away from the coalition by making bilateral commercial access more valuable than collective enforcement.<sup id="ref20"><a href="#fn20">[20]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION IX: WEAPONIZED INTERDEPENDENCE -->
<div class="section" id="s8">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">IX</span><h2>Weaponized Interdependence, Inverted</h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: The United States controls who can access the dollar system. Iran has built comparable, if more contested and less total, leverage over who can access Gulf transit at acceptable cost. The asymmetry of the Farrell-Newman model is partially reversed in physical space.</div>
  <p>Henry Farrell and Abraham Newman's concept of weaponized interdependence explains how states convert economic integration into coercive leverage — by exploiting the asymmetric position of hub nodes in networked economic systems to impose costs on peripheral actors.<sup id="ref21"><a href="#fn21">[21]</a></sup> Their framework was developed to explain American dollar dominance and the coercive potential of financial network centrality. It applies, with modifications, to the architecture of denial — but the asymmetry runs in the opposite direction.</p>
  <p>In the Farrell-Newman model, the United States is the hub: dollar-denominated trade, SWIFT transaction clearing, and correspondent banking dependencies create chokepoints through which Washington can impose costs on any state whose economic activity touches the dollar system. Iran has been the quintessential peripheral actor — subject to unilateral coercive pressure precisely because it cannot credibly threaten to exit the system that makes it vulnerable.</p>
  <p>The architecture of denial represents Iran's construction of a competing hub — not in the financial network but in the physical geography of global energy trade. The Strait of Hormuz carried approximately 14 million barrels per day of oil and condensates in pre-conflict conditions, roughly 26 percent of global maritime oil trade. That included half of Saudi and Emirati oil exports and nearly every LNG cargo from Qatar's Ras Laffan and Abu Dhabi's Das Island terminals.<sup id="ref25a"><a href="#fn25a">[25a]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-e">Estimated</span> No financial network can fully compensate for the physical absence of that energy, which is why the geographic chokepoint is treated here as a structurally distinct form of leverage from the financial one. The two are not equivalent, though: unlike the dollar system, Iran's control of Hormuz remains partial. It is contested by the U.S. naval presence and counter-blockade, and it depends on continued IRGC enforcement capacity rather than settled jurisdiction. The inversion is directional, not complete. Iran has gained leverage of a kind it did not previously possess — not a mirror image of the dollar system's totality.</p>
  <div class="voice-quote">
    <p>A regional power armed with cheap drones, mobile launchers, and the patience to keep the outcome in doubt has held a global chokepoint closed against the world's premier fleet, and sold safe passage through it to that fleet's competitors while doing so.</p>
    <cite>— LtCol James Jackson, USMC, "The Price of Doubt: Sea Control in the Strait of Hormuz," CIMSEC, June 22, 2026 <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></cite>
  </div>
  <p>On May 2, 2026, China's Ministry of Commerce operationalized its blocking statute framework — originally passed on paper in 2021 — to legally forbid Chinese entities from recognizing, enforcing, or complying with specific U.S. secondary sanctions. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The legal architecture now has two competing hubs: the dollar system and the Hormuz corridor. The PGSA sits at the intersection. LtCol Jackson's observation that Chinese- and Russian-flagged vessels transit the Strait "unmolested" under bilateral understandings with Tehran — "using the friction the U.S. Navy generates as a shield for their own commerce"<sup id="refJ1"><a href="#fnJ1">[J1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> — is a precise description of the weaponized interdependence inversion in operational terms: the United States is subsidizing its competitors' access to the corridor it cannot itself use.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION X: CEASEFIRE -->
<div class="section" id="s9">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">X</span><h2>The Ceasefire Precedent: Recognition Without Resolution</h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: The April 8 ceasefire did not require dismantling the toll regime. The June 18 Islamabad MOU operationalizes interaction with the PGSA and preserves Iran's right to resume charging. The architecture survived the agreement designed to address it.</div>
  <p>The April 8, 2026 ceasefire — announced by President Trump on Truth Social hours before U.S. bombers were reportedly already airborne, brokered by Pakistan — will be remembered, if the architecture of denial proves durable, not as the end of the conflict but as the moment the architecture was recognized. Trump stated he would "suspend the bombing and attack of Iran for a period of two weeks" subject to the "complete, immediate, and safe opening of the Strait of Hormuz." <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Iran agreed to a temporary reopening for the duration of negotiations. The toll regime was not required to be dismantled as a precondition. The Islamabad Talks of April 11–12 failed to produce an agreement, and the United States imposed a naval blockade on Iranian ports on April 13.<sup id="ref22"><a href="#fn22">[22]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <div class="pull-quote">
    <p>Whether or not the ceasefire holds, the world that emerges from this period is not the world that entered it. The toll road has been recognized. That recognition cannot be unrecognized.</p>
    <cite>— Author's analytical assessment <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></cite>
  </div>
  <p>Iran's Supreme National Security Council hailed the ceasefire as an American defeat. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The framing is strategically precise: a sitting U.S. president accepted a temporary pause without dismantling the governance regime that prompted the ultimatum. The architecture had been negotiated with, not over. The institutionalization dynamic is now self-reinforcing. Every day the toll regime operates — even under ceasefire conditions — it generates financial flows, builds institutional capacity, establishes legal precedents through arbitral and insurance proceedings, and deepens the de-dollarization infrastructure.</p>

  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 18 June 2026 — Islamabad MOU';">
    <p>On June 17–18, 2026, Presidents Trump and Pezeshkian signed the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding, brokered by Pakistan and Qatar, formally ending active hostilities. The MOU's treatment of the Strait of Hormuz confirms and extends the central argument advanced above.</p>
    <p><strong>The PGSA is named in the MOU.</strong> Iran's Supreme National Security Council statement implementing Paragraph 5 of the Islamabad MOU states explicitly: "commercial vessels requesting passage through the Strait of Hormuz must submit their application to the Persian Gulf Strait Administration (PGSA)."<sup id="ref26a"><a href="#fn26a">[26a]</a></sup> The United States entered a negotiating framework that operationalizes interaction with the PGSA rather than dissolving it. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>The toll is suspended, not the architecture.</strong> The SNSC statement specifies that "no fees will be charged from applicants for a period of sixty days, and these fees will be covered by the government of the Islamic Republic of Iran." Iran is paying the toll from its own budget during negotiations — the mechanism, the application process, and the PGSA's operational authority remain intact. Speaker Ghalibaf confirmed that post-negotiation passage fees are included in the MOU framework: Iran negotiated the right to resume charging as a written element of the agreement.<sup id="ref26c"><a href="#fn26c">[26c]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p><strong>The bilateral governance architecture is being institutionalized.</strong> Foreign Minister Araghchi stated publicly: "Iran will charge fees for services in the Strait of Hormuz. This mechanism and arrangements for managing the strait are being drafted... The management mechanisms for the Strait of Hormuz have largely been finalized with Oman."<sup id="ref26b"><a href="#fn26b">[26b]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p><strong>Iran has already re-closed the Strait once since the ceasefire.</strong> Tehran announced it had closed the strait in response to Israeli attacks in Lebanon. The U.S. and Iran subsequently agreed to establish a hotline and a coordination center for transit queries — a line of communication "to avoid incidents and miscommunication." Ghalibaf stated: "If the Americans have any concerns or if vessels require clarity on routes, they can reach out to us." <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> That framing — Iran as the answering authority for American routing inquiries — is the operational form of what is being called governance here.</p>
    <p><strong>Trump's toll statement.</strong> President Trump announced there would be no tolls during the 60-day negotiation period. He added that there would be no tolls afterward "unless they are imposed by and for the United States of America," stipulating that such tolls would be "for services rendered as the Guardian Angel to the countries of the Middle East for purposes of both past, present, and future reimbursement of costs."<sup id="refT1"><a href="#fnT1">[T1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The statement accepts the principle of a toll regime on an international strait, disputing only who collects the revenue. It is, analytically, a concession of the architecture's legitimacy while contesting its administration.</p>
    <p><strong>The architecture survived internal Iranian political resistance.</strong> Supreme Leader Khamenei publicly stated that he "held a different view" on the MOU and accepted it only because President Pezeshkian gave personal assurances and — according to Iranian sources — threatened to resign if permission was denied. The MOU passed the Supreme National Security Council on a second vote with approximately 65% support. The architecture was preserved even when Iran's own hardest-line actor accepted a deal he opposed. <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span></p>
    <p><strong>Analytical implication.</strong> The MOU is not a binding legal instrument and may not be implemented; the U.S. withdrew from the JCPOA under analogous circumstances.<sup id="ref26f"><a href="#fn26f">[26f]</a></sup> What the MOU demonstrates is that the U.S. entered a negotiating framework whose operative terms acknowledge rather than contest the PGSA's administrative function. The document that suspended the conflict names the PGSA as the transit coordination authority, preserves the right to resume charging, and initiates a 60-day window whose outcome will shape the governance architecture's permanent form — not determine whether one exists. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 29 June 2026 — Sole-Authority Claim and Domestic Pressure';">
    <p><strong>Araghchi claims exclusive, non-reviewable authority over the reopening.</strong> On June 29, 2026, Foreign Minister Araghchi posted a six-point statement asserting that the Strait will "return to its pre-war operating capacity within 30 days under the management adopted by Iran and after the obstacles are removed by the Islamic Republic of Iran," and that "responsibility for them rests solely with the Islamic Republic of Iran. No other institution or country bears responsibility in this regard."<sup id="refA1"><a href="#fnA1">[A1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> This is a materially stronger claim than anything analyzed in Sections V or VII above. It does not merely assert administrative jurisdiction over transit; it asserts that the very act of reopening — the restoration of "pre-war operating capacity" — is an Iranian unilateral prerogative, and that the 30-day clock is set by Iran's own assessment of when "obstacles" have been removed.</p>
    <p><strong>The statement explicitly forecloses parallel or competing arrangements.</strong> Araghchi warned that "any interference in this matter, or any attempt to establish new or separate arrangements from those currently being implemented by the Islamic Republic of Iran, will only complicate the situation, delay the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz, and increase tensions" — and cited recent incidents in the Strait as evidence that interference "has already contributed to rising tensions and confrontations."<sup id="refA1b"><a href="#fnA1">[A1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Read against the framework developed above, the statement functions as an explicit rejection of any rival governance architecture: not merely "we administer transit" (Section V) but "no other administration of transit is permitted to exist." The IRGC's parallel warning that it will "respond even more forcefully" to any perceived U.S. violation of the ceasefire — delivered by spokesperson Hossein Mohebi the same week — supplies the enforcement threat behind the diplomatic claim.<sup id="refA2"><a href="#fnA2">[A2]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p><strong>Iranian domestic politics confirms, rather than undercuts, the institutionalization thesis.</strong> A letter signed by 68 of the 88 members of Iran's Assembly of Experts — the clerical body empowered to appoint and remove the Supreme Leader — publicly pressured the MOU negotiators (President Pezeshkian, Speaker Ghalibaf, and FM Araghchi) to treat "the leadership's red lines" as a "religious obligation," and characterized the Strait's reopening, given continued Israeli operations in Lebanon, as "a strategic mistake" that would "make the enemy more bold."<sup id="refA3"><a href="#fnA3">[A3]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The letter explicitly lists "stabilizing the management of the Strait of Hormuz" alongside compensation, frozen assets, sanctions relief, and U.S. regional withdrawal as among the leadership's "inviolable demands." President Pezeshkian traveled to Qom — the Assembly's seat — to respond directly. <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span></p>
    <p><strong>Why this matters analytically.</strong> A skeptical reading might hold that the toll/governance architecture is simply a negotiating position — one Iran would readily trade away for sanctions relief and reconstruction assistance. The Assembly of Experts intervention undercuts that reading. It demonstrates that maintaining administrative control of Hormuz is not merely an executive-branch negotiating chip but a position with embedded domestic constituency support reaching into Iran's clerical establishment, expressed in explicitly religious-obligation terms. Separately, Majlis members have sought to pass a binding law mandating a Hormuz transit fee; the negotiating team has resisted that effort, precisely because statutory codification would eliminate their negotiating flexibility.<sup id="refA3b"><a href="#fnA3">[A3]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span> Both interventions cut the same direction as Section VII's claim about institutional depth: the architecture is no longer solely a creature of the negotiating team's discretion. It has accumulated enough domestic political investment that reversing it would now carry a domestic political cost independent of the external bargain reached with Washington. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 19 June 2026 — PGSA Insurance Mandate';">
    <p>Two days after the MOU was signed, the PGSA circulated a terms-and-conditions document to the shipping industry requiring all transiting vessels to carry insurance approved — and initially provided free — by the PGSA. The document, first reported by Lloyd's List and confirmed by Insurance Business and gCaptain, states: "The PGSA reserves the right to introduce insurance fees in the future… Owners will then be required to purchase and renew coverage accordingly."<sup id="ref27"><a href="#fn27">[27]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p><strong>The institutional move is more sophisticated than the original toll.</strong> The MOU guarantees "safe passage with no charge" for 60 days — language the U.S. interpreted as prohibiting transit fees. It says nothing about insurance premiums. By requiring PGSA-approved insurance as a condition of transit, Iran has established the PGSA as the <em>sole approving authority</em> for Hormuz transit insurance, displacing the International Group of P&amp;I Clubs, Lloyd's, and all existing underwriters. The precedent of mandatory PGSA insurance stands on day 61 regardless of whether fees are charged during the first 60 days. JD Vance, asked whether Washington would prevent future fees, said only that the U.S. believes "international waterways should be free of tolls" — not free of insurance premiums. The distinction is the architecture's next design feature. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>Three provisions in the PGSA T&amp;C document carry direct governance implications.</strong> First, the PGSA designates itself the sole body responsible for processing transit applications and issuing permits. Second, passage is permitted only via the designated route near Larak Island in Iranian waters; any deviation to the southern Omani corridor "will be treated as a violation." Third, the PGSA may "enforce penalties, revoke passage permissions, or take further legal action" for non-compliance. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p><strong>The OFAC sanctions trap.</strong> The PGSA was designated by OFAC on May 27, 2026. During the 60-day window no payment changes hands. After approximately August 17, 2026, if the PGSA introduces insurance fees — as it has explicitly reserved the right to do — a shipowner acquiring PGSA-approved insurance would be transacting with an OFAC-designated entity, creating direct liability under U.S. and EU sanctions law. Western commercial shipping through Hormuz may become structurally impossible without either sanctions relief or a framework that explicitly displaces the PGSA system. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>IMO engagement.</strong> IMO Secretary-General Arsenio Dominguez confirmed receipt of the PGSA T&amp;C document and stated that discussions are ongoing, having previously warned that transit fees "would set a dangerous precedent for other strategic waterways" — a principle he explicitly extended to mandatory insurance requirements imposed by a single coastal state. The IMO's engagement is itself a form of institutional recognition: a body that does not exist is not the subject of the Secretary-General's public concern. <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span> <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  </div>

  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 11 May &amp; 29 June 2026 — The Doctrine and the Bilateral Committee';">
    <p><strong>The "ten points" supply the strategic doctrine behind the operational architecture.</strong> On May 11, 2026, Iran's Supreme Leader's office circulated "ten strategic points regarding the Persian Gulf and the Strait of Hormuz," framing Gulf security as a matter to be "managed by the countries of the region themselves" and describing management of the Strait as "part of the formation of a new regional order."<sup id="refK1"><a href="#fnK1">[K1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Point seven is the clearest statement of the governance logic traced above through the PGSA's operational record: "Securing the Persian Gulf region is a practical expression of gratitude for Iran's management of the Strait of Hormuz" — language that frames Iranian administration of the chokepoint not as a wartime expedient but as an ongoing service to be reciprocated by regional states. Points eight through ten extend the same logic forward: preventing "hostile enemies" from exploiting the Strait, building "legal frameworks and a new system of management," and converting that management into a stream of "economic benefits to the Iranian people." Read together with Ghalibaf's June statement that Hormuz "will never return to its pre-war conditions" (Section VII above), the ten points supply the doctrinal register in which the PGSA's transit-authorization regime is presented domestically: not as a toll extracted under duress, but as the founding act of a new and permanent regional security order from which the United States is structurally excluded. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>The bilateral governance framework Section VII anticipated is now operating.</strong> On June 29, 2026, Iran and Oman held the first meeting of a joint Strait of Hormuz committee in Muscat, addressing "future management, maritime coordination, and navigational security." Iranian Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi stated that discussions emphasized "a governance framework that duly considers the rights and interests of all littoral states bordering the strategic waterway, within the framework of Article 5 of the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding."<sup id="refO1"><a href="#fnO1">[O1]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> This is, to date, the most concrete confirmation available of the claim advanced in Sections VI and VII: that the Iran-Oman bilateral arrangement, rather than remaining an informal diplomatic understanding, is being formalized into a standing joint committee with an explicit textual anchor in the MOU itself. The invocation of Article 5 also resolves an ambiguity flagged earlier: the publicly available summaries of the Islamabad MOU (Section X) referenced Paragraph 5 in connection with PGSA transit applications. The Iran-Oman committee's citation of the same provision indicates that Article/Paragraph 5 functions as the MOU's operative transit-governance clause more broadly, encompassing both the PGSA's authorization function and the bilateral littoral-state coordination function. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>Analytical significance.</strong> A standing bilateral committee, formally convened, citing a specific MOU provision, and addressed by a sitting Deputy Foreign Minister, is a categorically different institutional artifact than the informal "bilateral protocol" documented earlier only through diplomatic-contact and press reporting in Sections I and VI. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Tier evidence has now caught up with what was, as recently as April 2026, an <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span>-tier claim about the direction the architecture would take. The committee's stated agenda — governance "considering the rights and interests of all littoral states" — also signals that Iran is positioning the arrangement as multilaterally consultative in form even as Araghchi's June 29 statement (above) simultaneously asserts that Iran alone bears responsibility for the Strait's reopening. These two postures are not contradictory: the Oman channel formalizes consultation among littoral states while the U.S.-facing posture forecloses any role for non-littoral powers. The architecture is, in this sense, regionalizing its legitimacy claims at the same moment it is hardening its exclusionary ones.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 8 July 2026 — MOU Collapse and the Article 5 Dispute';">
    <p><strong>The Islamabad MOU collapsed on July 8, 2026 — in a manner that confirms rather than refutes the central argument above.</strong> A cascade of U.S. strikes hit Qeshm and Larak Islands and Bandar Abbas port. Iran retaliated with missiles and drones against U.S. facilities in Bahrain and Kuwait, downed a U.S. MQ-9 drone, and struck Saudi, Qatari, and UAE-linked tankers in the Strait. Trump declared at the NATO summit in Ankara: "I think it's over. I don't want to deal with them anymore." The U.S. simultaneously revoked the Iranian oil sanctions waiver that had been a core MOU provision, effective July 17.<sup id="refJ8a"><a href="#fnJ8a">[J8a]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

    <p><strong>The operational trigger was Article 5 — and its meaning is no longer in interpretive dispute.</strong> In the days before the collapse, Saudi, Qatari, and UAE-linked tankers attempted to transit via the southern Omani corridor under U.S. military overwatch without filing PGSA applications and without coordinating with Iran. This was not an accident or a commercial decision by individual shipowners. It was a deliberate U.S. strategy: escorting Gulf-state tankers through the non-PGSA route to contest Iranian jurisdictional control of the Strait while nominally remaining within the MOU's sixty-day window. The tactic was designed to demonstrate that the Omani corridor was usable without Iranian authorization — to make that a fact on the water before the MOU expired and fee charges resumed. Iran read the strategy correctly and fired on those tankers. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span> supported by: former French Ambassador Gérard Araud, who stated publicly that "at the first reading by any diplomat, the MoU was obviously giving Iran a free hand in the strait of Hormuz"<sup id="refJ8b"><a href="#fnJ8b">[J8b]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span>; Iranian lawmaker Alaeddin Boroujerdi, who stated that the agreement had established that shipping through the strait would take place "under the supervision and management of the Islamic Republic of Iran, specifically the IRGC Navy"<sup id="refJ8c"><a href="#fnJ8c">[J8c]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span>; and Iran's Foreign Ministry, which characterized the U.S. strikes and the simultaneous sanctions-waiver revocation as violations of MOU Articles 1 and 4, while affirming that Article 5 routing requirements remained operative regardless.<sup id="refJ8d"><a href="#fnJ8d">[J8d]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

    <p><strong>The SPR crisis explains why the U.S. signed the MOU — and why it could not afford to honor it.</strong> Trump publicly stated that U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserves — which stood at approximately 700 million barrels before the war — had been drawn down by approximately 400 million barrels during the conflict, leaving roughly 300 million barrels: a reserve he described as threatening the U.S. and world economies within weeks.<sup id="refJ8e"><a href="#fnJ8e">[J8e]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Total throughput via shadow transits — estimated by Kpler, JP Morgan, and Piper Sandler at between 1.9 and 2.9 million barrels per day — remained a fraction of the pre-war 15.6 million barrel daily baseline.<sup id="refJ8f"><a href="#fnJ8f">[J8f]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> The MOU's primary function was to address a U.S. reserve and oil price emergency. It was not a good-faith attempt to resolve the governance dispute over Hormuz. The United States needed the Strait open, signed an agreement that acknowledged Iranian administrative authority over it, and then immediately began contesting that authority operationally — because accepting Iranian control permanently was politically and strategically intolerable, but so was another month without oil. The contradiction was always there. The July 8 collapse is what it looks like when a contradiction between strategic necessity and strategic interest becomes kinetic.</p>

    <p><strong>The Oman fracture.</strong> Oman condemned Iran's strikes on shipping and on Bahrain and Kuwait, affirmed solidarity with neighboring states, and urged all parties to exercise "self-control" and return to diplomacy.<sup id="refJ8g"><a href="#fnJ8g">[J8g]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> This is Oman's first public break with Iran during the conflict. It matters structurally. Section VII identified Oman's bilateral cooperation — including the June 29 joint committee invoked under Article 5 — as one of the three conditions converting the IRGC control map from assertion into operative practice. Oman has not dissolved the committee. It has not repudiated Article 5. But its public censure of Iranian strikes on shipping demonstrates that the bilateral architecture rests on Oman's tolerance of Iranian primacy, not on its active endorsement of it — and that tolerance has limits. Whether the joint committee survives this breach is the specific, observable test that will determine whether Iran's cartographic-sovereignty argument retains its Omani anchor.</p>

    <p><strong>The architecture has outlasted the agreement about it.</strong> As of July 8, despite Trump's declaration that the MOU is "over," the United States has not reinstated the April 13 naval blockade of Iranian ports. The U.S. revoked the oil sanctions waiver but did not attempt to interdict Iranian oil at sea. Iran has not stood down the PGSA. The PGSA's transit-authorization infrastructure — the application process, the Larak Island corridor, the insurance mandate, the control map — remains in place. The governance architecture that the MOU named and legitimized in June continues to function in July, without the MOU. The paper's central claim — that the architecture of denial would outlast the debate about it — has now survived not just a ceasefire but the ceasefire's collapse.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'UPDATE · 9 July 2026 — Oman Tells the IMO It Rejects Transit Fees';">
    <p><strong>Oman has moved from tolerating the architecture to contesting its legal premise directly, in the one forum built to adjudicate it.</strong> In a statement to the IMO, Oman's Foreign Ministry said: "The Sultanate of Oman reiterates that the right of transit passage through straits used for international navigation is guaranteed under international law," and that "Oman remains fully committed to these legal principles and does not support the imposition of transit fees on vessels passing through the Strait of Hormuz."<sup id="refO2"><a href="#fnO2">[O2]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Foreign Minister Badr Al Busaidi repeated the same position in an interview with Monte Carlo Doualiya Radio the same week, corroborated independently by Oman Observer and AFP: Oman "does not support" charging vessels to use the strait, while leaving open voluntary maritime-service arrangements — navigational safety, emergency response, pollution control — modeled on the Malacca and Singapore Straits.<sup id="refO3"><a href="#fnO3">[O3]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p><strong>This is a stronger fact than anything Section VII's "Condition one" previously credited to Oman.</strong> That condition treated the June 29 Iran-Oman joint committee as evidence of third-party behavioral conformity — Oman acting as though the claimed zone were operative, "without contesting the claimed boundaries." An on-record legal objection lodged directly with the IMO is not that. It is Oman using the one international body positioned to receive such an objection to reject, by name, the compulsory-fee logic that is the toll regime's core administrative content. The joint committee has not been dissolved and Article 5 has not been repudiated, so the institutional relationship Section VII documented still exists — but "conformity without contestation" no longer accurately describes Oman's position. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
    <p><strong>The distinction Oman is drawing — mandatory tolls versus voluntary service fees — is the same distinction drawn above between blockade and toll road, run in the opposite direction.</strong> Where the tollbooth-as-governance framing treats the PGSA's fee schedule as evidence of administrative legitimacy, Oman's framing treats any compulsory charge as definitionally incompatible with transit passage, full stop — leaving only voluntary, opt-in service arrangements as lawful. CNBC's reporting captures the resulting ambiguity precisely: analysts describe Oman's position as deliberate "strategic ambiguity," tacking between flat rejection of tolls and openness to fees "called something other than a toll."<sup id="refO4"><a href="#fnO4">[O4]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span> Whether a service-cost mechanism ultimately adopted under Omani sponsorship functions, in practice, as a toll by another name is exactly the question Section V argues cannot be settled by the label a state chooses for it.</p>
    <p><strong>A further legal wrinkle Oman's statement does not resolve: UNCLOS binds only its parties, and Oman's ratification cannot make transit passage binding on Iran's territorial sea.</strong> Oman has ratified UNCLOS; Iran has not.<sup id="ref13c"><a href="#fn13c">[13c]</a></sup> UNCLOS's transit-passage regime applies, as a treaty matter, only to states that have accepted it — and only within the territorial waters of states that are party to it. Oman can bind itself. It cannot, by invoking UNCLOS, bind Iran's territorial waters to a regime Iran has not ratified, nor can it unilaterally internationalize the portions of the strait that fall under Iranian sovereignty. Whether transit passage nonetheless applies as customary international law binding on non-parties is a genuinely contested question — Washington has long argued exactly that, as Section VI notes — but it is not resolved simply by Oman's assertion that the right is "guaranteed under international law." Oman's statement is, in this narrow but important sense, the mirror image of Iran's own overreach: where Iran's control map asserts administrative jurisdiction over waters it does not fully control, Oman's IMO statement asserts a legal guarantee over waters — Iran's — that Oman's own ratification cannot bind. <span class="ev tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  </div>
</div>
<!-- SECTION XI: INDUSTRY RECKONING -->
<div class="section" id="s9b">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">XI</span><h2>The Industry Reckoning: When Sea Control Becomes Actuarial</h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: The maritime industry's response to the Hormuz crisis is independent external validation of the argument developed above. Lloyd's, Intertanko, and the shipping lines are not behaving as they would in a blockade. They are behaving as they would with a regulated passage.</div>
  <p>The response of the maritime industry to the Hormuz crisis is worth examining on its own terms, because it arrives from an epistemically independent direction: not from strategic analysis but from actuarial practice.</p>
  <div class="voice-quote">
    <p>I don't believe the traffic through the Strait of Hormuz will return to normal before the end of the year.</p>
    <cite>— Richard Meade, Editor-in-Chief, Lloyd's List, June 2026 <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></cite>
  </div>
  <p>Meade's statement is analytically significant not as a prediction but as a pricing signal. The editor-in-chief of Lloyd's List is not a naval strategist; he is the authoritative voice of the world's premier maritime risk publication. When he projects a twelve-month timeline for normalization after a ceasefire, he is communicating the market's assessment of structural, not tactical, disruption. A blockade that ends produces normalization over days; a governance architecture that has changed the conditions of transit produces normalization on a scale measured in institutional and regulatory adjustment — which takes longer, if it comes at all.</p>
  <div class="voice-quote">
    <p>Even if the ceasefire holds, around 10% of global container shipping capacity is impacted by the blockade and freight rates are spiraling across major trades. This scale of disruption and market volatility cannot be reversed overnight.</p>
    <cite>— Peter Sand, Chief Analyst, Xeneta, June 2026 <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></cite>
  </div>
  <div class="voice-quote">
    <p>Tolls for infrastructure such as the Suez or Panama canals are different, as they reflect major infrastructure investments. That's not the case in the Strait of Hormuz.</p>
    <cite>— Hapag-Lloyd spokesperson, to The Guardian, June 2026 <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></cite>
  </div>
  <p>The Hapag-Lloyd statement is the most revealing. The spokesperson does not say tolls are impermissible in principle; the company operates through the Suez and Panama canals, both of which charge transit fees. The objection is specifically that Hormuz tolls "reflect no major infrastructure investment" — a legal and economic argument about the <em>basis</em> for the charge, not a categorical rejection of the toll concept. It is the objection of a company that has already mentally modeled the Hormuz toll as a potential business line-item and found the legal justification wanting, not the objection of a company that considers the concept categorically outside the realm of commercial reality.</p>
  <div class="voice-quote">
    <p>The main route… through the middle of the Strait of Hormuz, that's closed, that's dangerous. This is like a highway where the road in the middle is closed, and you are using the hard shoulder. We need to get the highway open so we can get the volume of traffic through safely.</p>
    <cite>— Phil Belcher, Marine Director, Intertanko, June 2026 <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></cite>
  </div>
  <p>Belcher's "hard shoulder" metaphor is the maritime industry's own spontaneous articulation of the tollbooth-as-governance framework. A highway with a closed central lane and an open hard shoulder is not a war zone — it is a managed road with unusual traffic conditions. The hard shoulder is navigable; traffic is moving; the question is when the highway reopens, not whether it exists. Intertanko's concern is operational continuity — the hard-shoulder routes through the Larak Island corridor (Iranian waters) and the southern Omani route lack the capacity of the central passage, requiring vessels to navigate close to rocks and significantly limiting throughput. The problem Intertanko is describing is not a military problem. It is an infrastructure management problem. And the entity managing the infrastructure, whether Intertanko has formally acknowledged it as such or not, is the PGSA.</p>
  <div class="ext-validation" data-label="External Validation · CIMSEC / LtCol James Jackson, USMC · June 22, 2026">
    <p>LtCol James Jackson's June 22, 2026 CIMSEC analysis — "The Price of Doubt: Sea Control in the Strait of Hormuz" — offers a complementary theoretical framework from within the U.S. military's own analytical tradition that substantively converges with the argument here, while arriving from a different disciplinary direction.</p>
    <p>Jackson's core claim: <strong>"Command of the sea has slipped from the gun line to the insurance ledger."</strong> He defines sea control as having become "an actuarial condition: whether the strait can be used is decided by the price underwriters put on the risk of crossing it — the same arithmetic an actuary applies to any hazard — not by which navy wins the day's engagement." This is the military's own acknowledgment that the decisive variable in a chokepoint contest is the toll schedule, not the intercept rate.</p>
    <p>Jackson observes that <strong>Chinese- and Russian-flagged vessels transit unmolested</strong> under bilateral understandings with Tehran — "using the friction the U.S. Navy generates as a shield for their own commerce." He further notes that the Red Sea precedent confirms the structural claim: container traffic through Suez fell roughly 75% in 2024 and "had not recovered through mid-2025, persisting even through pauses in Houthi activity." The mechanism that kept the Red Sea closed during lulls in Houthi attacks is the same mechanism keeping Hormuz administratively restricted during the ceasefire: the market prices persistence of threat, not the intercept rate.</p>
    <p>Jackson does not use the term "tollbooth as governance architecture." But his policy prescription — that the decisive maritime weapon is "a credible guarantee, a predictable corridor, and a visible ending" — implicitly accepts that the toll regime has converted the strait from a commons into a priced passage, and that the U.S. response must operate on the same terrain. <strong>A guarantee is a price instrument. A predictable corridor is a managed transit schedule.</strong> The prescription accepts the architecture's terms even in rejecting its current administration.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION XII: THE PGSA -->
<div class="section" id="s10">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">XII</span><h2>The PGSA and What Comes Next</h2></div>
  <div class="section-claim">Key claim: The PGSA's first ninety days were a reasonable test of whether the architecture would collapse under naval, insurance, or diplomatic pressure. The conditions that would dislodge it have not occurred; on two of three measures, the trend runs the other way.</div>
  <div class="update-box" style="--update-label: 'OPERATIONAL RECORD · May–June 2026';">
    <p>On May 5, 2026, Iran formally established the Persian Gulf Strait Authority (PGSA).<sup id="ref23"><a href="#fn23">[23]</a></sup> The PGSA launched a public X account on May 18 and began processing applications through info@PGSA.ir, requiring vessel owners to submit ownership records, flag registration, cargo manifests, crew lists, and AIS data before receiving a transit permit and corridor coordinates. The ABC News investigation (May 9, 2026) independently confirmed the PGSA's application process is operational: responding to an ABC application request, the IRGC provided a 40-field Excel spreadsheet covering vessel name, cargo type and value, current and previous flag, nationality of owner and operators, and direction of travel, with instructions to submit for "compliance review." <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
    <p>By early June, the PGSA reported processing over 300 non-Iranian transit requests — 77% outbound, primarily to China and India.<sup id="ref23b"><a href="#fn23">[23]</a></sup> The U.S. Treasury's OFAC sanctioned the PGSA on May 27, 2026, characterizing it as an IRGC-linked extortion mechanism. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent described it as "the Iranian military's latest attempt to extort global maritime trade."<sup id="ref12ab"><a href="#fn12a">[12a]</a></sup> <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> Iran's Foreign Ministry rejected the extortion characterization, describing the fees as payment for navigational services and environmental protection. <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span></p>
    <p>The Majlis full-chamber vote on the "Plan for the Security and Development of the Persian Gulf and the Strait of Hormuz" remained pending as of early June, with a senior lawmaker stating the parliament's decision to legislate was "final and permanent" — and that management of the Strait was "not tactical or temporary." <span class="ev tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  </div>
  <p>The OFAC designation is analytically useful for a narrower reason than is sometimes claimed: it confirms that the PGSA exists as an organized entity with traceable personnel, infrastructure, and a wallet/payment apparatus sufficient to meet OFAC's designation threshold — not that it has any legal standing. The PGSA is now on the SDN list, the same list that houses entities the United States considers illegitimate by definition; sanctions designation is evidence of operational reality, not of recognition in any normative sense, and is not treated as such here. The more precise claim is this: OFAC's designation, combined with the EU's parallel sanctioning of named IRGC-Navy individuals (Section VI) and the PGSA's continued operation despite both, demonstrates that the architecture has proven durable under the specific form of pressure — financial sanctions — that has historically been the West's primary non-military lever against Iranian state entities. That durability is the fact worth noting; "recognition" is the wrong word for it.</p>

