  <p>None of this should be read as a claim that the architecture is durable beyond challenge — only that it has, so far, displayed the operational signature of an institutionalized regime under conditions that might have been expected to dislodge it. The clearest failure condition is sustained multilateral naval enforcement that physically interdicts PGSA-cleared vessels — the U.S. counter-blockade targeted Iranian ports, not the transiting vessels the PGSA processes; a shift to the latter would directly contest the system's core function. A second is insurer refusal: if major underwriters collectively declined to cover Hormuz transits irrespective of PGSA clearance, the underlying voyage would become unfinanceable. A third is defection by major non-adversary users: if China or India concluded the diplomatic costs of routing through a sanctioned authority outweighed the savings, the throughput that gives the architecture its institutional character would collapse. None of these three conditions occurred in the first ninety days.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION XIII: CONCLUSION -->
<div class="section" id="s11">
  <div class="section-head"><span class="section-num">XIII</span><h2>What the Governance Model Demands</h2></div>
  <p>The policy implications follow directly from the central argument. If the architecture of denial is a governance architecture rather than a military instrument, then the appropriate response is not military defeat but institutional engagement — which requires first accepting the uncomfortable acknowledgment that there is a governing authority to engage with.</p>
  <p>This is not a counsel of capitulation. It is a counsel of analytical honesty. The institutions currently formulating responses to the Hormuz situation — the IMO, Lloyd's, the P5, the U.S. Navy's Fifth Fleet — are operating with doctrines designed for blockades. They need doctrines for toll roads. The legal community needs a framework for the novel form of maritime jurisdiction the Iran-Oman bilateral protocol has created, and for the cartographic jurisdictional claim the IRGC control map embodies. The insurance industry needs actuarial models accounting for administered transit regimes — including the PGSA's position as a mandatory insurance approving authority — rather than episodic interdiction risk. The financial community needs to assess what a durable PGSA crypto clearinghouse means for the petrodollar architecture's medium-term integrity.</p>
  <p>Ghalibaf's statement that "the Strait of Hormuz will never return to its pre-war conditions" should be read carefully rather than taken at face value or dismissed as propaganda. What is verifiably true, independent of Ghalibaf's framing, is narrower than what the statement claims: the PGSA exists, processes applications, issues insurance requirements, and has been named in the document that suspended the conflict. <span class="ev tier-c">Confirmed</span> What remains genuinely open is whether that operational continuity will harden into something resembling settled governance or will instead persist as contested, coercive practice indefinitely — a state of affairs that resembles neither the pre-war status quo nor a stabilized new order. The Walvis Bay and shadow-fleet precedents discussed in Section V suggest such intermediate states can last for years without resolution either way. The question the Islamabad MOU has operationalized is not whether the PGSA currently functions — it does — but whether its function will ever be regularized into law, abandoned under sufficient pressure, or simply continue indefinitely as an unresolved fact on the water.</p>
  <p>And above all, the analytical community needs to stop looking at the Stena Impero and seeing only a grievance. Whatever motivated the seizure, the operational capability it demonstrated was refined across six subsequent years — the Asphalt Princess, the Advantage Sweet, the corridor enforcement architecture of 2026, the IRGC control map of May 4 — in a progression read here as demonstration becoming doctrine, doctrine becoming cartographic claim, and cartographic claim becoming — at least provisionally, and subject to the conditions specified in Section VII — operative practice. The architecture has been built, cartographically extended, partially sanctioned into international recognition of its existence, named in a bilateral agreement, and found to be processing transit requests and issuing insurance mandates while the negotiations about its future are underway — none of which settles whether it will prove permanent.</p>
  <p>The events of late June 2026 sharpened this picture. The events of July 8, 2026 completed it.</p>

  <p>The Islamabad MOU collapsed because the United States signed an agreement acknowledging Iranian administrative authority over the Strait of Hormuz and then immediately began contesting that authority operationally &#8212; escorting Gulf-state tankers through the non-PGSA corridor to make transit without Iranian permission a fact on the water. The contradiction was not incidental to U.S. strategy. It was U.S. strategy. Washington needed the Strait open to address a reserve and oil price emergency; it could not afford to accept Iranian control permanently; so it accepted it on paper and contested it in practice. Iran recognized the strategy, attacked the escorted tankers, and the MOU was over. What remains &#8212; the PGSA, the Larak Island corridor, the insurance mandate, the control map, the Iran-Oman joint committee &#8212; is the architecture. The United States has not reinstated its naval blockade. What the MOU legitimized in June is still operating in July, without the MOU behind it.</p>

  <p>None of this is foreordained going forward. The U.S. counter-blockade could be reinstated. Iran could overplay its hand. Oman&#8217;s public break with Iran over the July 8 strikes introduces new uncertainty into the bilateral architecture. The conditions under which the governance claim could fail remain live. But the specific, observable test identified in Section XII has now run: the MOU collapsed, the naval blockade was not reinstated, and the PGSA kept processing. That test has a result.</p>

  <p>If history records the spring and summer of 2026 clearly, it will record three things: that the Strait of Hormuz was converted from a commons into a toll road, that the United States signed an agreement naming the tollkeeper&#8217;s administrative body as the operative transit authority and then tried to break the toll regime through operational contestation rather than legal challenge, and that the architecture survived both the agreement and its collapse. The Architecture of Denial will outlast the debate about it.</p>
  <p style="font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.72rem;color:var(--muted);letter-spacing:0.04em;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding-top:1rem;margin-top:2rem;">This is Paper II of the Chokepoint Geopolitics Research series.<sup id="ref-priority"><a href="#fn-priority">[†]</a></sup> Paper I — <em>The Tungsten Clock: Operation Epic Fury and the Material Limits of American War</em> — is available on SSRN and via the Chokepoint Geopolitics GitHub repository.</p>
</div>
<!-- GLOSSARY -->
<div class="glossary" id="glossary">
  <span class="glossary-label">Terms Used in This Paper</span>
  <dl class="glossary-grid">
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Architecture of Denial</dt><dd>This paper's framing for Iran's coupled maritime control system at Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb — administered as a governance regime rather than conducted as a wartime blockade.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Tollbooth as Governance Architecture</dt><dd>The paper's central analytical contribution: Iran's Hormuz transit regime functions as administrative regulation — requiring negotiation with the governing authority, not its defeat.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Cartographic Sovereignty</dt><dd>This paper's term for the conditional process by which the IRGC's May 4, 2026 control map — a 22,000+ sq km claim including portions of Oman's and the UAE's territorial waters — converts from unilateral assertion into operative practice. The conversion depends on third-party behavioral conformity, the absence of contestation that changes routing facts, and integration with the PGSA's authorization infrastructure — not on the map's legal validity, which remains contested and unresolved.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Economic Denial Regime</dt><dd>A permanent, institutionalized system of controlled access to a strategic corridor, distinct from both blockade and freedom-of-navigation doctrine — the operational form the tollbooth architecture takes.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Coupled Chokepoint System</dt><dd>The conditional claim that Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb interact multiplicatively rather than additively — specifically when the Cape of Good Hope detour is available but costly, and a vessel's route requires both passages. Under those conditions, disruption at one chokepoint forecloses the cost-mitigation option the other would otherwise provide.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Weaponized Interdependence</dt><dd>Farrell and Newman's framework describing how hub actors in networked economic systems convert integration into coercive leverage. Applied here in inverted form: Iran as the geographic hub, using physical geography rather than financial network position.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>PGSA (Persian Gulf Strait Authority)</dt><dd>Iranian government agency, founded May 5, 2026, administering transit authorization through the Strait of Hormuz. Sanctioned by OFAC May 27, 2026. Named in Paragraph 5 of the Islamabad MOU, June 18, 2026. Issued mandatory insurance requirement June 19, 2026.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Ostrom Inversion</dt><dd>The reversal of Elinor Ostrom's commons-governance logic: rather than managing a common-pool resource for collective benefit, Iran has converted a global commons into a controlled-access toll regime.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Institutional Depth</dt><dd>The accretion of precedent, infrastructure, and embedded interest that makes a new institutional arrangement increasingly costly to reverse — even for actors with nominal power to do so.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Actuarial Sea Control</dt><dd>LtCol Jackson's formulation (CIMSEC, June 2026): "whether the strait can be used is decided by the price underwriters put on the risk of crossing it." The mechanism by which the PGSA's insurance mandate converts corridor governance into a financial instrument independent of kinetic control.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Sole-Authority Claim</dt><dd>FM Araghchi's June 29, 2026 assertion that reopening the Strait is an exclusive Iranian prerogative on a 30-day, Iran-set timeline, and that any "separate arrangements" by other parties will be treated as interference. A categorically stronger claim than administrative jurisdiction over transit alone.</dd></div>
    <div class="gloss-item"><dt>Iran-Oman Strait of Hormuz Committee</dt><dd>Standing bilateral body, first convened in Muscat on June 29, 2026, explicitly invoked under Article 5 of the Islamabad MOU. The formal institutionalization of the bilateral governance arrangement traced from Section I onward. Its continued operation should not be read as Omani acceptance of compulsory transit fees — see Oman's July 2026 IMO statement, Section X.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</div>

