  <p class="fn" id="fn9"><a href="#ref9" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>9</sup> Precise strike-performance data from Operation Epic Fury remains classified. The inference that actual performance exceeded official pre-conflict estimates rests on publicly reported U.S. and allied asset repositioning orders and statements by unnamed allied officials acknowledging that Iranian precision-strike performance "exceeded expectations." <span class="tier tier-e">Estimated</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn10"><a href="#ref10" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>10</sup> The coupled chokepoint model is the author's analytical construct. Its structural logic draws on complex systems theory (Perrow, <em>Normal Accidents</em>) and maritime network analysis (Stopford, <em>Maritime Economics</em>, 4th ed.). See also Abel Meza et al., "Disruption of Maritime Trade Chokepoints and the Global LNG Trade," <em>Maritime Transport Research</em> 3 (2022); Ulrichsen and Krane, "Maritime Chokepoints and Risks to Global Shipping and Energy Security," Rice University Baker Institute for Public Policy, 2026. External validation: LtCol Jackson, CIMSEC, June 22, 2026. <span class="tier tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn11"><a href="#ref11" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>11</sup> Iranian Foreign Ministry ten-point peace framework, circulated March 2026. Reported by Al-Monitor, Tasnim, and the Tehran Times. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn11a"><a href="#ref11a" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>11a</sup> Elinor Ostrom, <em>Governing the Commons: The Evolution of Institutions for Collective Action</em> (Cambridge University Press, 1990). Garrett Hardin, "The Tragedy of the Commons," <em>Science</em> 162, no. 3859 (1968): 1243–1248. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn11b"><a href="#ref11b" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>11b</sup> The "Ostrom inversion" as applied to Hormuz is the author's analytical construct. See David Bollier and Silke Helfrich, eds., <em>The Wealth of the Commons</em> (Levellers Press, 2012), on the distinction between commons-enclosure by privatization and by administrative exclusion. <span class="tier tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn11c"><a href="#ref11c" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>11c</sup> UN Security Council Resolution 432 (17 July 1978) on Walvis Bay. Transfer of sovereignty to Namibia effective 1 March 1994. Oxford Public International Law (Max Planck Encyclopedia), "Walvis Bay." <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn12"><a href="#ref12" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>12</sup> TRM Labs, "Iranian Crypto Tolls in Strait of Hormuz," April 10, 2026. Documented oil tanker fees at $0.50–$1 per barrel, translating to approximately $2M per VLCC transit. Payment options: Chinese yuan, Bitcoin, USDT via PGSA-linked wallets. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn12a"><a href="#ref12a" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>12a</sup> U.S. Department of the Treasury, "Economic Fury Targets Iranian Maritime Extortion," press release, May 27, 2026, <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0507" target="_blank" rel="noopener">home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0507</a>. OFAC SDN listing: <a href="https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20260527_33" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20260527_33</a>. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn13"><a href="#ref13" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>13</sup> The Iran-Oman bilateral Strait governance protocol, as a claimed jurisdictional basis distinct from UNCLOS, is referenced in Araghchi's June 18, 2026 statement that "management mechanisms for the Strait of Hormuz have largely been finalized with Oman" (see fn26b) and is treated here as an assertion by an interested party rather than an independently verified legal instrument. <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn13a"><a href="#ref13a" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>13a</sup> UNCLOS (1982), Part II Section 3 (innocent passage, Arts. 17–26) and Part III Section 2 (transit passage, Art. 38 and Art. 44, which prohibits suspension of transit passage). <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn13b"><a href="#ref13b" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>13b</sup> Corfu Channel (United Kingdom v. Albania), Merits, ICJ Judgment of 9 April 1949. Full text via <a href="https://www.icj-cij.org/case/1/judgments" target="_blank" rel="noopener">icj-cij.org/case/1/judgments</a>. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn13c"><a href="#ref13c" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>13c</sup> The National, "The 21-mile gap in international law that could reshape the global order," April 7, 2026. <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn14"><a href="#ref14" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>14</sup> The Independent, March 25, 2026, citing lawmaker Mohammadreza Rezaei Kouchi via Fars and Tasnim: "We provide its security, and it is natural that ships and oil tankers should pay such fees." <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn15"><a href="#ref15" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>15</sup> House of Saud analysis, "Iran Hormuz Toll Law: What the Strait Fee Means for Gulf Oil," April 5, 2026. <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn16"><a href="#ref16" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>16</sup> James Kraska, <em>Maritime Power and the Law of the Sea</em> (Oxford University Press, 2011). <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn17"><a href="#ref17" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>17</sup> Jason Chuah, City University London, cited in Lloyd's List Intelligence analysis, March–April 2026; Kennedys Law, "Possible implications of Iranian tolls to transit the Strait of Hormuz," April 10, 2026. Lee Harris et al., "Insurers to Cancel Policies and Raise Prices for Ships in Gulf and Strait of Hormuz," <em>Financial Times</em>, February 28, 2026. Julia Kollewe, "Lloyds of London Stresses it is Still Insuring Shipping in Strait of Hormuz," <em>The Guardian</em>, March 11, 2026. Ulrichsen and Krane (Baker Institute, 2026) note war risk premiums rose to 1–1.5% of insured value from a pre-conflict average of 0.25%. <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn18"><a href="#ref18" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>18</sup> The de-dollarization implication of yuan/crypto-denominated Hormuz tolls is the author's inference. On petrodollar architecture origins: David Spiro, <em>The Hidden Hand of American Hegemony</em> (Cornell, 1999). <span class="tier tier-i">Analyst Inference</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn19"><a href="#ref19" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>19</sup> TRM Labs, April 2026: IRGC previously routed approximately $1B through offshore stablecoin infrastructure. OFAC designated Zedcex and Zedxion in January 2026 in connection with IRGC stablecoin operations. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn20"><a href="#ref20" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>20</sup> House of Saud analysis, April 5, 2026, on CMA CGM Kribi transit and Security Council veto timing. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn21"><a href="#ref21" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>21</sup> Henry Farrell and Abraham Newman, "Weaponized Interdependence: How Global Economic Networks Shape State Coercion," <em>International Security</em> 44, no. 1 (Summer 2019): 42–79, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1162/ISEC_a_00351" target="_blank" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.1162/ISEC_a_00351</a>. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn22"><a href="#ref22" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>22</sup> CFR analysis, "As a Strait of Hormuz Standoff Grows, Will Trump's Fragile Iran Ceasefire Hold?"; House of Commons Library Research Briefing CBP-10637, updated June 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn23"><a href="#ref23" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>23</sup> PGSA establishment: Wikipedia, "Persian Gulf Strait Authority," founded May 5, 2026, public operations from May 18. OFAC SDN designation, May 27, 2026. 300+ transit requests: GlobalSecurity.org citing PressTV, June 1, 2026. ABC News investigation (Emily Clark and Tavleen Singh), "Iran is trying to formalise its control over the Strait of Hormuz, but there are some obvious problems," May 9, 2026 — independently confirmed operational PGSA application process via email exchange with info@PGSA.ir, receiving 40-field Excel spreadsheet. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn25a"><a href="#ref25a" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>25a</sup> Ulrichsen and Krane, "Maritime Chokepoints and Risks to Global Shipping and Energy Security," Rice University Baker Institute for Public Policy, 2026; Rystad Energy, "Chokepoints under Pressure," September 8, 2025; 2025 Statistical Review of World Energy, Energy Institute, 74th Edition. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn26a"><a href="#ref26a" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>26a</sup> Iran Supreme National Security Council statement implementing Paragraph 5 of the Islamabad MOU, June 18, 2026. Reported by Al Mayadeen and Middle East Spectator, June 18, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn26b"><a href="#ref26b" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>26b</sup> Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, press statement, June 18, 2026. Sources: Al Mayadeen, IRNA, Fars, Tasnim, Mehr, June 18, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn26c"><a href="#ref26c" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>26c</sup> Iranian Parliament Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf, televised interview, June 17–18, 2026; Ghalibaf Telegram video. Supreme Leader Khamenei's acceptance under protest and SNSC vote at approximately 65% support: Al Mayadeen, IRNA, Tasnim, Mehr, June 18, 2026. <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn26f"><a href="#ref26f" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>26f</sup> On the non-binding character of the Islamabad MOU and the JCPOA precedent: U.S. State Department, "Ceasing U.S. Participation in the JCPOA," May 8, 2018. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fn27"><a href="#ref27" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>27</sup> Lloyd's List, "Iran imposes mandatory insurance on ships transiting Strait of Hormuz, with fees likely to follow," LL1157571, June 19, 2026. Confirmed by: Insurance Business, June 19, 2026; gCaptain; Fortune, "Iran floats 'insurance fees' and asserts control over Hormuz," June 20, 2026. Phil Belcher, Intertanko: "The main route… through the middle of the Strait of Hormuz, that's closed, that's dangerous… This is like a highway where the road in the middle is closed, and you are using the hard shoulder." Richard Meade, Lloyd's List: does not believe traffic will return to normal before end of 2026. Peter Sand, Xeneta: "around 10% of global container shipping capacity is impacted." Hapag-Lloyd: "fundamentally wrong" to charge vessels; "Tolls for infrastructure such as the Suez or Panama canals are different, as they reflect major infrastructure investments. That's not the case in the Strait of Hormuz." INTERTANKO Managing Director Tim Wilkins stated the PGSA directive "went far beyond what the MOU contemplated." Lloyd's and Chubb launched a new Hormuz war risk consortium on June 19, offering $400 million in combined capacity. War risk premiums peaked at 1.5–3% of hull value, from a pre-crisis baseline of approximately 0.2%. The 60-day MOU window expires approximately August 17, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnM1"><a href="#refM1" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>M1</sup> Mohammad Akbarzadeh, Deputy Political Director, IRGC Navy, quoted in Fars News Agency, May 4, 2026, reported by multiple outlets including ABC News, "Iran just redrew the Strait of Hormuz. New IRGC map shows why the world is worried," May 12, 2026: "In the past, the Strait of Hormuz was defined as a limited area around islands such as Hormuz and Hengam, but today this view has changed." Zone extends from Jask (east) to Siri Island (west); effective width expanded from approximately 32–48 km to 322–483 km per Iranian media reports. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnM4"><a href="#refM4" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>M4</sup> Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf, video posted to Telegram account on return from Buergenstock talks, June 2026: "The Strait of Hormuz will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran, in accordance with international law." Also: "I was among the first to assert at the onset of the war that the management of the Strait of Hormuz will never return to its former state." Reported by IRNA, Fars, Tasnim, Al Mayadeen, and multiple wire services. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnM5"><a href="#refM5" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>M5</sup> Phil Belcher, Marine Director, Intertanko, cited in The Guardian, Lloyd's List, ABC News and multiple outlets, June 2026. The 80-mine estimate for the central passage is reported by Lloyd's List; mine-clearance timeline has not been established. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnT1"><a href="#refT1" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>T1</sup> President Donald Trump, statement on Strait of Hormuz tolls, June 2026: no tolls during the 60-day negotiation period; no tolls afterward "unless they are imposed by and for the United States of America" for "services rendered as the Guardian Angel to the countries of the Middle East for purposes of both past, present, and future reimbursement of costs." Reported by The Guardian and multiple outlets. JD Vance said only that the U.S. believes "international waterways should be free of tolls" — not free of insurance premiums. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnJ1"><a href="#refJ1" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J1</sup> LtCol James Jackson, USMC, "The Price of Doubt: Sea Control in the Strait of Hormuz," Center for International Maritime Security (CIMSEC), June 22, 2026. Jackson: "Chinese-flagged and Russian-flagged vessels move through it under bilateral understandings with Tehran, using the friction the U.S. Navy generates as a shield for their own commerce." Also: "The United States, sustaining a high-risk environment from which it has exempted its two principal competitors, is paying the full premium to buy Beijing and Moscow a discount." LtCol Jackson is a career logistician in the U.S. Marines and an operational and strategic planner assigned to US Cyber Command, and a graduate of the Maritime Advanced Warfighting School at the U.S. Naval War College. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnA1"><a href="#refA1" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>A1</sup> Iran's Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi (@araghchi), statement posted via the Iranian Embassy in Slovenia and Iranian diplomatic social media accounts, June 29, 2026: "Under the memorandum of understanding, the Strait of Hormuz will return to its pre-war operating capacity within 30 days under the management adopted by Iran and after the obstacles are removed by the Islamic Republic of Iran. These arrangements are currently being implemented, and responsibility for them rests solely with the Islamic Republic of Iran. No other institution or country bears responsibility in this regard. According to the memorandum of understanding signed between Iran and the United States, any interference in this matter, or any attempt to establish new or separate arrangements from those currently being implemented by the Islamic Republic of Iran, will only complicate the situation, delay the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz, and increase tensions." <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnA2"><a href="#refA2" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>A2</sup> Hossein Mohebi, IRGC spokesperson, statement to state-run SNN TV, late June 2026, reported via Al Jazeera live coverage: "As we predicted, the enemy is an enemy that breaks its commitments, is deceitful, and cannot be trusted... If the enemy breaks its commitments and violates the ceasefire, we will respond more strongly than before, and we stress we will respond even more forcefully." Reported amid continuing tit-for-tat strikes in the Gulf. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnA3"><a href="#refA3" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>A3</sup> Statement of the Assembly of Experts (68 of 88 members), published in Farsi and English, late June 2026, widely circulated including via the social media account @Soureh_design2. Key passage: "considering the continuation of the aggressors and crimes of the filthy Zionist regime in Lebanon and the failure to withdraw from the occupied territories, which is a clear violation of the first clause of the memorandum of understanding, the opening of the Strait of Hormuz is contrary to the obligations of the officials and is considered a strategic mistake." The statement lists "stabilizing the management of the Strait of Hormuz" among the leadership's "inviolable demands." President Pezeshkian's subsequent visit to Qom, and Majlis members' efforts to pass a binding Hormuz transit-fee law (resisted by the negotiating team to preserve flexibility), are reported in the same analytical roundup. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span> (statement text) / <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span> (Majlis fee-law efforts and Qom visit)</p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnK1"><a href="#refK1" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>K1</sup> "10 Strategic Points by Iran's Supreme Leader on the Persian Gulf and the Strait of Hormuz," WANA News, May 11, 2026. The ten points, attributed to remarks by Seyed Mojtaba Khamenei in his capacity within the Supreme Leader's office: (1) U.S. forces are "the main source of insecurity" in the Gulf; (2) American bases cannot guarantee their own security; (3) the Gulf has "a bright future without the United States"; (4) Iran and its neighbors "share a common destiny"; (5) hostile foreign powers belong only "at the bottom of [the Gulf's] waters"; (6) Iran's "chain of victories" signals "a new regional and global order"; (7) "Securing the Persian Gulf region is a practical expression of gratitude for Iran's management of the Strait of Hormuz"; (8) preventing "hostile enemies from exploiting the Strait of Hormuz"; (9) "legal frameworks and a new system of management for the Strait of Hormuz"; (10) "economic benefits to the Iranian people through the new management of the Strait of Hormuz." <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnO1"><a href="#refO1" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>O1</sup> "Joint Iran-Oman committee discusses Strait of Hormuz coordination," Al Mayadeen English, June 29, 2026, 18:23. First meeting of the joint Oman-Iran Strait of Hormuz committee held in Muscat. Iranian Deputy Foreign Minister for Legal and International Affairs Kazem Gharibabadi, quoted: discussions emphasized "a governance framework that duly considers the rights and interests of all littoral states bordering the strategic waterway, within the framework of Article 5 of the Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding." The Omani Ministry of Foreign Affairs separately confirmed the meeting addressed "frameworks for cooperation in maritime navigation, given that both countries are littoral states of the Strait of Hormuz." <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnO2"><a href="#refO2" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>O2</sup> Oman Ministry of Foreign Affairs statement to the International Maritime Organization, reported in "Oman Tells UN It's Against Imposing Transit Fees in Hormuz," Bloomberg, July 9, 2026: "The Sultanate of Oman reiterates that the right of transit passage through straits used for international navigation is guaranteed under international law... Oman remains fully committed to these legal principles and does not support the imposition of transit fees on vessels passing through the Strait of Hormuz." <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnO3"><a href="#refO3" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>O3</sup> Foreign Minister Sayyid Badr bin Hamad Al Busaidi, interview with Monte Carlo Doualiya Radio, late June 2026, published by Oman's Foreign Ministry June 29, 2026. Corroborated by Oman Observer, "Oman rejects transit fees on Strait of Hormuz ships," June 30, 2026, and AFP, "Oman rejects transit fees for ships using Strait of Hormuz," July 2026. Al Busaidi drew a distinction between compulsory tolls, which Oman opposes as "internationally prohibited," and voluntary maritime-service mechanisms modeled on the Strait of Malacca and Singapore Strait. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnO4"><a href="#refO4" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>O4</sup> CNBC, "Oman walks a diplomatic tightrope over Strait of Hormuz fees, creating a 'blind spot' for markets," July 3, 2026. Dania Thafer (Gulf International Forum) and Andrew Leber (Carnegie Middle East Program) both characterize Oman's position as deliberately ambiguous, tacking between rejecting tolls outright and floating fees framed as maritime services rather than transit charges. <span class="tier tier-r">Reported</span></p>
  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8a"><a href="#refJ8a" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8a</sup> Trump declaration at NATO summit in Ankara, July 8, 2026: "I think it's over. I don't want to deal with them anymore... They're scum." U.S. strikes on Qeshm Island, Larak Island, and Bandar Abbas port: U.S. Central Command, July 8, 2026. Iranian retaliation — 85 targets in Bahrain and Kuwait, MQ-9 drone downed: IRGC statement via PressTV/IRNA, July 8, 2026. U.S. revocation of Iranian oil sanctions waiver effective July 17: U.S. Treasury, July 8, 2026. Sources: Naked Capitalism, "Iran War: Trump Declares MOU Over," July 8, 2026; Al Jazeera live feed, July 8, 2026; Bloomberg, "Trump Says US Ceasefire With Iran Is 'Over' After Strikes," July 8, 2026; NBC News live blog, July 8, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8b"><a href="#refJ8b" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8b</sup> Gérard Araud (@GerardAraud), post on X, 19:59 UTC, July 7, 2026: "At the first reading by any diplomat, the MoU was obviously giving Iran a free hand in the strait of Hormuz." Araud served as French Ambassador to the United States (2014–2019) and to Israel, and as Permanent Representative to the United Nations. He has no institutional stake in the U.S.-Iran dispute. His reading is cited here as independent expert corroboration of the legal analysis of Article 5's operative meaning developed above, not as a claim by a party to the agreement. Source: Moon of Alabama, "War On Iran: Both Sides Declare Their MoU To Be Ineffective," July 8, 2026 (citing Araud's X post). <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8c"><a href="#refJ8c" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8c</sup> Alaeddin Boroujerdi, member of the Iranian Parliament's National Security and Foreign Policy Committee, July 8, 2026: the agreement and talks had established that shipping through the strait would take place "under the supervision and management of the Islamic Republic of Iran, specifically the IRGC Navy." Source: Naked Capitalism, "Iran War: Trump Declares MOU Over," July 8, 2026 (citing Moon of Alabama/Iran International). <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8d"><a href="#refJ8d" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8d</sup> Iran Foreign Ministry statement, July 8, 2026: U.S. strikes constituted "a flagrant violation" of MOU Article 1 (cessation of military operations); the sanctions waiver revocation violated Article 4 arrangements; continued Israeli aggression in Lebanon had rendered "important and fundamental parts" of the agreement ineffective. "The responsibility for the dangerous consequences of this escalation rests with the treaty-breaking US regime." Source: IRNA, July 8, 2026; Naked Capitalism, "Iran War: Trump Declares MOU Over," July 8, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8e"><a href="#refJ8e" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8e</sup> Trump's public statements on U.S. Strategic Petroleum Reserves: pre-war level approximately 700 million barrels; approximately 400 million barrels consumed during the conflict; approximately 300 million barrels remaining. The Guardian, "Has the US really carried out a secret mission to get oil through Hormuz?," June 12, 2026, confirms SPR drawdown of 6.2 million barrels in one week alone. Bloomberg, July 8, 2026, confirms oil prices surged over 6% on July 8 following the MOU collapse. Brent crude had previously fallen from a peak near $125/barrel in late April to approximately $93/barrel before the escalation, suggesting partial relief from shadow transits. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8f"><a href="#refJ8f" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8f</sup> Shadow transit and throughput estimates: Lloyd's List Intelligence confirmed approximately 25% of Gulf tankers present at the start of the crisis had exited since conflict began; 36 transits recorded June 1–7, of which 17 were "dark" (AIS off). Kpler: approximately 1.9 million barrels per day average since April. JP Morgan: highs of 2.1 million barrels per day in late May. Piper Sandler: up to 2.9 million barrels per day. Pre-war baseline: 15.6 million barrels per day (Joint Maritime Information Center). Trump's claimed figure of 200 vessels and 100 million barrels "cannot be fully factchecked" but is "broadly consistent" with Kpler's observed crude flows (The Guardian, June 12, 2026). Source: The Guardian, "Has the US really carried out a secret mission to get oil through Hormuz?," June 12, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fnJ8g"><a href="#refJ8g" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>J8g</sup> Oman Foreign Ministry statement, July 8, 2026, condemning Iran's attacks on sites in Bahrain and Kuwait and on two commercial ships from Saudi Arabia and Qatar in the Strait of Hormuz. The statement affirmed Oman's solidarity with neighboring countries and said "increasing military tensions in the region threaten Oman's safety and security." Oman urged all parties to exercise "self-control," refrain from escalation, and return to diplomacy. This is the first public condemnation by Oman of Iranian military action during the conflict. UAE diplomatic adviser Anwar Gargash stated Iran's actions showed it was "incapable of adhering to the requirements of de-escalation." Sources: Al Jazeera live feed, July 8, 2026; Gulf News live feed, July 8, 2026; Naked Capitalism, "Iran War: Trump Declares MOU Over," July 8, 2026. <span class="tier tier-c">Confirmed</span></p>

  <p class="fn" id="fn-priority"><a href="#ref-priority" class="fn-backlink">↩</a><sup>†</sup> This paper is the second in the Chokepoint Geopolitics Research series. Paper I — <em>The Tungsten Clock: Operation Epic Fury and the Material Limits of American War</em> — argues that the conflict exposed a structural contradiction between American doctrine of global primacy and an industrial base unable to reproduce its material conditions. Both papers apply Farrell and Newman's weaponized interdependence framework to physical rather than financial networks — material chokepoints in Paper I, maritime geography in Paper II. The "tollbooth as governance architecture" framing was developed prior to Iran's own adoption of governance-register language in the March 30 Majlis bill and Araghchi's juridical framing.</p>
